@* WHY CascadingAuthenticationState WRAPS THE ROUTER
   -------------------------------------------------
   <AuthorizeRouteView> and every <AuthorizeView>/[Authorize] in the app read the current user from an
   AuthenticationState that must be *cascaded* down the component tree. CascadingAuthenticationState is the
   provider that subscribes to our JwtAuthenticationStateProvider and pushes the ClaimsPrincipal down as a
   cascading value. Without it, authorization components have no state to read and treat everyone as
   anonymous — or throw. It belongs here at the very root so it covers all routed pages.

   WHY <NotAuthorized> RENDERS A COMPONENT INSTEAD OF NAVIGATING INLINE
   The previous version called Navigation.NavigateTo(...) directly inside this markup. That executes during
   the render pass, which Blazor interprets as a framework auth challenge and then demands IAuthenticationService
   (part of the server cookie-auth stack we don't register), throwing at runtime. Delegating to
   <RedirectToLogin>, which navigates in OnAfterRender, moves the redirect *out* of the render pass and fixes it.
   
   WHY AuthStateInitializer IS INSIDE THE CASCADE
   In InteractiveServer mode with prerender:false, the SignalR circuit establishes AFTER the initial render,
   so JS interop (localStorage) isn't ready when GetAuthenticationStateAsync first runs. AuthStateInitializer
   waits for the circuit to be fully live, then re-triggers authentication state evaluation so the token can
   be successfully read from localStorage. This fixes "logged out after refresh" and "blank page after login". *@
<CascadingAuthenticationState>
    <AuthStateInitializer />
    <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
        <Found Context="routeData">
            <AuthorizeRouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)">
                <NotAuthorized>
                    @* Shown briefly while RedirectToLogin bounces an unauthenticated visitor to /login. *@
                    <RedirectToLogin />
                </NotAuthorized>
                <Authorizing>
                    @* Rendered while the auth state is still resolving (e.g., decoding the JWT), so the
                       user sees a spinner rather than a flash of "not authorized" content. *@
                    <MudProgressCircular Color="Color.Primary" Indeterminate="true" Class="ma-8" />
                </Authorizing>
            </AuthorizeRouteView>
            <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
        </Found>
    </Router>
</CascadingAuthenticationState>
